※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A floating-point literal says what it is with a suffix, as an integer literal
※ does.  Both of the forms C has are written the same way here: a decimal one
※ with a point or an exponent, and a hexadecimal one with `0x` and `p`, which
※ is exact by construction and is why C has it.

@[visible]
let pi: f64 = 3.14159f64

@[visible]
let half: f32 = 0.5f32

@[visible]
let exactly_twelve: f64 = 0x1.8p3f64

@[visible]
let large: f64 = 1e10f64

@[visible]
let whole: f64 = 3f64

@[visible]
let negative: f32 = ⁻2.5f32

@[startup]
fn main() → u8:
    0u8
